
[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What's the default for a field that isn't explicitly classified? → Deny by default (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more before I present a design. This one sets the record shape, which is the part that's expensive to revisit.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how much can you tell, from the log store, that two records came from the same person?

  - [1mEphemeral session ID.[0m A random UUID minted per page load (and per process start in [38;5;153msrc/[39m), held in memory only. Lets
    you reconstruct "what did this session do before it broke," which is most of what you want during an incident. Can't
    tell you a user hits the same bug every Tuesday.
  - [1mPersistent anonymous device ID.[0m Same, but stored in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m so it survives reloads. You get recurring-failure
    patterns per user. It's also, unambiguously, a persistent tracking identifier — that pulls in consent, a documented
    retention period, and a reset path.
  - [1mNo correlation ID at all.[0m Simplest. With more than one user hitting the endpoint, interleaved records can't be
    untangled into sequences, which removes most of the diagnostic value you're paying the remote sink for.

  Asymmetry again: adding persistence later is an additive field change. Removing a persistent ID after the fact means
  purging what you've already stored and explaining why you collected it.

  [1mMy recommendation: ephemeral session ID.[0m It buys the sequence reconstruction that makes production debugging work, and
  it doesn't make you a data controller for a tracking identifier.
